course: everyday
lesson: 387
title: Ponytail tugging: crushes shown clumsily
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every morning in the line for assembly, Tom tugged Lily's ponytail. To Tom it felt like a game; to Lily it hurt a bit and annoyed her a lot. When his friends started chanting «Tom loves Lily!», Tom went bright red and pulled harder, just to prove he didn't. Lily's hair got a knot, she cried, and Tom felt awful all afternoon.
explain: Lily complained at home, and her mum said: «Oh, it just means he likes you.» Lily felt nobody was listening. Then her dad said: «Liking someone is not a reason to hurt them,» and Mum agreed she had been wrong. At the same time Tom's gran asked him why he kept pulling the girl's hair. Tom stared at his shoes and admitted he didn't know how to talk to her. «Then say something nice,» said Gran, «like "I like your drawing".»
explain: Tom's first sorry went badly: he said «I didn't mean it» and laughed, and Lily could tell it wasn't real. The next day he tried again: «I'm sorry for pulling your hair. I won't do it again.» And he didn't. Meanwhile Ollie hid Lily's pencil case three days running, until Lily said: «Just say hello instead, it's easier.» Ollie went as red as a tomato. Later he tried skateboard tricks to impress her, fell off and scraped his knee, and Lily asked if he was OK. It was the first real talk they ever had. Maddie had her own version: two boys from different streets kept throwing her hat into a tree, until she told them, «If you like me, just say so.»
explain: Mrs Okafor saw what was going on and gave a whole lesson called «How to show you like someone». Everybody giggled, but the class made a list: say hello, share a snack, ask about their day, notice their drawing, never pull hair. A crush shown by hurting or annoying someone is still hurting, and it is the worst way to make someone like you.
fact: Some children who like someone show it clumsily, by teasing, tugging or hiding their things.
fact: Pulling hair hurts, even when the person doing it thinks it is a game.
fact: «It means he likes you» is never a reason to put up with being hurt.
fact: Teasing someone about a crush can push them into doing unkind things to hide it.
fact: Telling a teacher when someone keeps hurting you is right, not telling tales.
fact: A real apology names what you did, is said without laughing, and is kept.
fact: Kind words, like a compliment or a hello, work far better than tugs and tricks.
fact: Showing off to impress someone often ends with a fall or an embarrassment.
fact: Annoying someone makes them want to stay away, not get closer.
fact: Adults can help children find the right words for their feelings.
exercise: How did Tom feel about tugging Lily's ponytail at first? | He thought it was a game | He wanted to make her cry | He was trying to tidy her hair | He was cross with her
exercise: How did Lily feel about the tugging? | Annoyed and a bit hurt | Pleased that Tom noticed her | Bored and did not notice | Proud that the boys liked her
exercise: Why did Tom pull harder when his friends chanted «Tom loves Lily»? | To prove to them that he didn't like her | Because Lily had asked him to | Because the teacher wasn't looking at the time | Because he wanted to win a dare from his friends
exercise: What happened after Tom pulled harder? | Lily's hair got a knot and she cried | Lily laughed and pulled his hair back | Lily's ponytail came off | Nothing, because Lily didn't feel it
exercise: Why was Lily's mum wrong to say «it means he likes you»? | Liking someone is no reason to hurt them | Tom did not really like Lily at all | Mums should never talk about crushes with children | Lily was too young to complain
exercise: What did Tom admit to his gran? | He didn't know how to talk to Lily | He hated Lily's ponytail | His friends paid him to do it | Lily had pulled his hair first at break
exercise: What did Gran advise Tom to do instead? | Say something nice, like praising her drawing | Ignore Lily completely for the rest of the year | Pull her hair, but more gently | Ask his friends what to say
exercise: Why was «I didn't mean it» followed by a laugh not a real apology? | A real sorry is serious and names what you did | Apologies must always be written down to count | He should have said it in front of the whole class | Only adults can make real apologies
exercise: What made Tom's second apology real? | He said what he did, promised to stop and kept his word | He bought Lily a present from the shop | He made all his friends say sorry to her too | He said it very loudly in assembly so everyone heard
exercise: Why did Ollie keep hiding Lily's pencil case? | To get her attention | To borrow her felt-tip pens | Because he had lost his own | Because Mrs Okafor told him to
exercise: What did Lily tell Ollie? | «Just say hello instead, it's easier» | «Leave me alone or I'll hit you» | «Give it back or you're dead, I mean it» | «I'm telling your mum on you»
exercise: How did Ollie's skateboard tricks end? | He fell, scraped his knee and Lily asked if he was OK | He did a perfect flip and Lily clapped | Lily laughed at him and walked off | He won a big trophy and Lily was really impressed
exercise: Two boys from different streets kept throwing Maddie's hat into a tree. What did she say? | «If you like me, just say so» | «I'll punch you both tomorrow» | «My hat is ugly anyway» | «I'm calling the police»
exercise: What did the class list as good ways to show you like someone? | Say hello, share a snack, ask about their day | Hide their things, pull their hair, tease them | Show off, swear, do dangerous tricks | Stare at them and say nothing
exercise: Why do children sometimes tease or tug instead of being kind? | They feel shy and don't know the right words | They want the person to dislike them | They have been told it is polite | They think it makes them look grown-up and cool
exercise: Which is braver than pulling someone's hair? | Saying something kind to them face to face | Pulling it when nobody is looking | Getting a friend to pull it for you instead | Hiding their pencil case instead
test: «tom pulls my hair EVERY day in the line». Mum: «aw, he fancies you». What's true? | Liking you doesn't make hurting you OK | She should be flattered and say nothing | She should pull his hair back harder | It will stop on its own if she smiles
test: Tom's mates: «oooh Tom loves Lily!!» If Tom wants to look cool, what's the wise move? | Shrug, say «so what» and leave her hair alone | Pull her hair to show he doesn't | Push the boy who said it | Call Lily names so they believe he doesn't like her
test: «soz didn't mean it lol». Is that a real apology? | No, it laughs it off and promises nothing | Yes, he said soz | Yes, apologies don't need to be serious to count | No, because he didn't buy a present
test: Ollie hid her pencil case again. What would have worked better for Ollie? | Just saying hello to her | Hiding her lunchbox as well | Writing on her pencil case | Telling everyone she lost it
test: Teacher asks: «Lily, why didn't you tell me sooner?» Lily: «I didn't want to tell tales.» What's true? | Telling about being hurt every day isn't telling tales | Telling a teacher always makes it worse | She should have sorted it with her fists | It's only telling tales if a girl does it to a boy
test: Ollie's skateboard, big ollie, face-plant. Lily walks over. What's the kind thing? | Ask if he's OK | Film it and post it | Laugh and walk off | Tell him he's rubbish
test: «chucked maddie's hat in the tree again haha». Why might the boys be doing it? | It might be a clumsy crush, but it still isn't OK | They want to help her climb | Hats in trees are lucky | They are trying to be her bodyguards on the way home
test: Gran: «Why d'you keep pulling that girl's hair, pet?» What's the honest answer Tom gave? | He didn't know how to talk to her | She asked him to do it | Everybody does it, Gran, it's normal | Her hair was in his way
test: «just ignore it, boys are like that». Is that good advice for Lily? | No, she can say stop and tell a teacher | Yes, girls must just put up with it | Yes, it shows he respects her | No, she should cut her hair off short
test: Mrs Okafor writes «How to show you like someone» on the board and the class giggles. Which belongs on the list? | Notice their drawing and say it's good | Grab their ponytail in the line | Throw their hat into a tree | Show off with a dangerous trick
exam: Identify the principal reason for which certain children manifest affection by means of teasing or the pulling of hair. | They lack the words to express the sentiment and feel embarrassed | They intend to cause the other child lasting distress and unhappiness | They have been instructed so to act by adults | Such conduct is a recognised token of courtesy
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the assertion «it signifies that he likes you» when offered in response to a complaint of hair-pulling? | It does not render the hurtful conduct acceptable | It constitutes a sufficient reason to tolerate the conduct | It establishes that the conduct is affectionate and harmless | It obliges the complainant to reciprocate the affection
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established features of a genuine apology. | It names the wrong committed, is earnest, and is subsequently honoured | It is accompanied by laughter in order to lessen the tension | It denies any intention whatsoever | It is delivered solely in the presence of witnesses
exam: It is given that a pupil wishes to signify friendly regard for a classmate. State the conduct most conducive to that end. | A greeting, a kind remark or the sharing of a snack | The concealment of the classmate's belongings | The repeated pulling of the classmate's hair | A hazardous display of skill before the classmate in the playground
